+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> For the same 1-million-row dataset, CSV takes 100 MB, gzip-compressed CSV drops to 18 MB, JSON balloons to 145 MB (due to key repetition), and Parquet compresses to 22 MB while also preserving column types. Parquet is the clear winner for machine-to-machine data exchange.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Best practice:</strong> Save intermediate results as Parquet, share with non-technical stakeholders as Excel, publish to web as JSON, and use CSV only for maximum compatibility. Always pass <code>index=False</code> unless the index is meaningful.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 5: Data Type Optimization
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">5. Data Type Optimization</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>After loading, you can still reduce memory by 60-90%.</strong> Pandas defaults to the largest types (int64, float64, object). Most real data fits comfortably in smaller types. This section shows four key techniques: category dtype, numeric downcasting, sparse arrays, and the string type.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Technique 1: Category dtype</strong> -- for columns with few unique values (low cardinality):</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># Simulate a 1M-row dataset</span>\nnp.random.<span class="fn">seed</span>(<span class="num">42</span>)\nn = <span class="num">1_000_000</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({\n    <span class="str">"country"</span>: np.random.<span class="fn">choice</span>([<span class="str">"US"</span>, <span class="str">"UK"</span>, <span class="str">"DE"</span>, <span class="str">"FR"</span>, <span class="str">"JP"</span>], n),\n    <span class="str">"status"</span>: np.random.<span class="fn">choice</span>([<span class="str">"active"</span>, <span class="str">"inactive"</span>, <span class="str">"pending"</span>], n),\n    <span class="str">"price"</span>: np.random.<span class="fn">uniform</span>(<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">1000</span>, n),\n    <span class="str">"quantity"</span>: np.random.<span class="fn">randint</span>(<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">100</span>, n)\n})\n\n<span class="cm"># Before optimization</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Before:"</span>, df.<span class="fn">memory_usage</span>(deep=<span class="kw">True</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>() / <span class="num">1e6</span>, <span class="str">"MB"</span>)\n<span class="cm"># ~130 MB</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Convert low-cardinality strings to category</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"country"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"country"</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"category"</span>)\ndf[<span class="str">"status"</span>] = df[<span class="str">"status"</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"category"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Downcast numerics</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"price"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[<span class="str">"price"</span>], downcast=<span class="str">"float"</span>)  <span class="cm"># float64 -> float32</span>\ndf[<span class="str">"quantity"</span>] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[<span class="str">"quantity"</span>], downcast=<span class="str">"integer"</span>)  <span class="cm"># int64 -> int8</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># After optimization</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"After:"</span>, df.<span class="fn">memory_usage</span>(deep=<span class="kw">True</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>() / <span class="num">1e6</span>, <span class="str">"MB"</span>)\n<span class="cm"># ~7 MB -- 95% reduction!</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Technique 2: Automatic optimization function</strong> -- apply to any DataFrame:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">optimize_dtypes</span>(df, cat_threshold=<span class="num">0.05</span>):\n    <span class="str">"""Reduce DataFrame memory by optimizing dtypes."""</span>\n    <span class="kw">for</span> col <span class="kw">in</span> df.columns:\n        col_type = df[col].dtype\n        \n        <span class="kw">if</span> col_type == <span class="str">"object"</span>:\n            <span class="cm"># Convert to category if few unique values</span>\n            ratio = df[col].<span class="fn">nunique</span>() / <span class="fn">len</span>(df)\n            <span class="kw">if</span> ratio < cat_threshold:\n                df[col] = df[col].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"category"</span>)\n        \n        <span class="kw">elif</span> np.<span class="fn">issubdtype</span>(col_type, np.integer):\n            df[col] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[col], downcast=<span class="str">"integer"</span>)\n        \n        <span class="kw">elif</span> np.<span class="fn">issubdtype</span>(col_type, np.floating):\n            df[col] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[col], downcast=<span class="str">"float"</span>)\n    \n    <span class="kw">return</span> df\n\n<span class="cm"># Usage</span>\ndf = pd.<span class="fn">read_csv</span>(<span class="str">"data.csv"</span>)\nbefore = df.<span class="fn">memory_usage</span>(deep=<span class="kw">True</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>()\ndf = <span class="fn">optimize_dtypes</span>(df)\nafter = df.<span class="fn">memory_usage</span>(deep=<span class="kw">True</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>()\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Reduced {before/1e6:.1f} MB -> {after/1e6:.1f} MB"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Savings: {(1 - after/before)*100:.0f}%"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Memory savings chart EN --- */
+ '<div id="plot-savings-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
+ '<script>setTimeout(function(){'
+ 'var data=[{x:["Original","category\\nconversion","+ numeric\\ndowncast","+ string\\ntype"],y:[130,14,7,6.5],type:"bar",marker:{color:["#f87171","#c8a96e","#4ecdc4","#4de87a"],line:{color:"rgba(255,255,255,0.2)",width:1}},text:["130 MB","14 MB","7 MB","6.5 MB"],textposition:"outside",textfont:{color:"#ebe6dc",size:12}}];'
+ 'var layout={title:{text:"Cumulative Memory Savings (1M Rows, 4 Columns)",font:{color:"#ebe6dc",size:14}},paper_bgcolor:"rgba(0,0,0,0)",plot_bgcolor:"rgba(0,0,0,0)",font:{color:"#ebe6dc"},yaxis:{title:"Memory (MB)",gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.06)",zerolinecolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.15)"},xaxis:{gridcolor:"rgba(255,255,255,0.06)"},margin:{t:50,r:30,b:70,l:60},showlegend:false};'
+ 'Plotly.newPlot("plot-savings-en",data,layout,{responsive:true,displayModeBar:false});'
+ '},200)</script>'

+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> Starting from 130 MB for a naive 1M-row DataFrame, converting strings to category reduces to 14 MB, adding numeric downcasting brings it to 7 MB, and using the string dtype drops it to 6.5 MB. That is a 95% total reduction from a few lines of code.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps"><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Category dtype</div><div class="step-detail">For columns with <5% unique values (country, status, color). Stores integers internally with a small lookup table. Savings: 90-99% for string columns.</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Numeric downcasting</div><div class="step-detail"><code>pd.to_numeric(col, downcast="integer")</code> picks the smallest type that fits: int64 -> int32 -> int16 -> int8. Same for floats.</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">String dtype</div><div class="step-detail"><code>df["col"] = df["col"].astype("string")</code> uses PyArrow-backed strings -- more memory efficient and faster than object dtype.</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Sparse arrays</div><div class="step-detail"><code>df["col"] = df["col"].astype(pd.SparseDtype("float", fill_value=0))</code>. For columns that are mostly zeros (NLP features, one-hot encoding). Only stores non-zero values.</div></div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">THINK ABOUT IT</div><div class="think-body"><strong>When does optimization matter?</strong> For datasets under 100 MB, don\'t bother -- just load and work. For 100 MB to 1 GB, optimize dtypes after loading. For 1 GB+, combine usecols + dtype at load time + chunking. For 10 GB+, switch to Dask, Polars, or a database -- Pandas was not designed for that scale.</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 6: Real-World Pipeline
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">6. Real-World Pipeline</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Theory meets practice.</strong> Real CSV files have wrong encodings, messy column names, mixed delimiters, and inconsistent null values. This section shows a complete pipeline: load a messy file, fix encoding, clean column names, handle nulls, optimize types, and export clean data.</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pandas <span class="kw">as</span> pd\n<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Step 1: Handle encoding ----</span>\n<span class="cm"># Common encodings: utf-8, latin-1, cp1252, iso-8859-1</span>\n<span class="kw">try</span>:\n    df = pd.<span class="fn">read_csv</span>(<span class="str">"messy_data.csv"</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>)\n<span class="kw">except</span> UnicodeDecodeError:\n    df = pd.<span class="fn">read_csv</span>(<span class="str">"messy_data.csv"</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"latin-1"</span>)\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Fell back to latin-1 encoding"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Step 2: Fix column names ----</span>\n<span class="cm"># "  Order ID ", "Product Name", "TOTAL $" -> clean snake_case</span>\ndf.columns = (df.columns\n    .<span class="fn">str</span>.<span class="fn">strip</span>()           <span class="cm"># remove leading/trailing spaces</span>\n    .<span class="fn">str</span>.<span class="fn">lower</span>()           <span class="cm"># lowercase</span>\n    .<span class="fn">str</span>.<span class="fn">replace</span>(<span class="str">r"[^a-z0-9]+"</span>, <span class="str">"_"</span>, regex=<span class="kw">True</span>)   <span class="cm"># special chars -> _</span>\n    .<span class="fn">str</span>.<span class="fn">strip</span>(<span class="str">"_"</span>)        <span class="cm"># remove leading/trailing underscores</span>\n)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.columns.<span class="fn">tolist</span>())\n<span class="cm"># [\'order_id\', \'product_name\', \'total\']</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Step 3: Initial inspection ----</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.shape)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">dtypes</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">isnull</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>())  <span class="cm"># null count per column</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(df.<span class="fn">duplicated</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>())  <span class="cm"># duplicate rows</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># ---- Step 4: Handle missing values ----</span>\n<span class="cm"># Drop rows where ALL values are NaN</span>\ndf = df.<span class="fn">dropna</span>(how=<span class="str">"all"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Fill numeric NaNs with median</span>\nfor col <span class="kw">in</span> df.<span class="fn">select_dtypes</span>(include=<span class="str">"number"</span>).columns:\n    df[col] = df[col].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(df[col].<span class="fn">median</span>())\n\n<span class="cm"># Fill categorical NaNs with "Unknown"</span>\nfor col <span class="kw">in</span> df.<span class="fn">select_dtypes</span>(include=<span class="str">"object"</span>).columns:\n    df[col] = df[col].<span class="fn">fillna</span>(<span class="str">"Unknown"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Step 5: Remove duplicates ----</span>\ndf = df.<span class="fn">drop_duplicates</span>()\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Step 6: Fix data types ----</span>\n<span class="cm"># Price stored as "$1,234.56" string -> float</span>\n<span class="kw">if</span> df[<span class="str">"total"</span>].dtype == <span class="str">"object"</span>:\n    df[<span class="str">"total"</span>] = (df[<span class="str">"total"</span>]\n        .<span class="fn">str</span>.<span class="fn">replace</span>(<span class="str">"$"</span>, <span class="str">""</span>, regex=<span class="kw">False</span>)\n        .<span class="fn">str</span>.<span class="fn">replace</span>(<span class="str">","</span>, <span class="str">""</span>, regex=<span class="kw">False</span>)\n        .<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="fn">float</span>))\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Step 7: Optimize dtypes ----</span>\nfor col <span class="kw">in</span> df.<span class="fn">select_dtypes</span>(include=<span class="str">"object"</span>).columns:\n    <span class="kw">if</span> df[col].<span class="fn">nunique</span>() / <span class="fn">len</span>(df) < <span class="num">0.05</span>:\n        df[col] = df[col].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="str">"category"</span>)\n\nfor col <span class="kw">in</span> df.<span class="fn">select_dtypes</span>(include=<span class="str">"float"</span>).columns:\n    df[col] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[col], downcast=<span class="str">"float"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># ---- Step 8: Export clean data ----</span>\ndf.<span class="fn">to_parquet</span>(<span class="str">"clean_data.parquet"</span>)\ndf.<span class="fn">to_csv</span>(<span class="str">"clean_data.csv"</span>, index=<span class="kw">False</span>)\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Final shape: {df.shape}"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Memory: {df.memory_usage(deep=True).sum() / 1e6:.1f} MB"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps"><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Encoding</div><div class="step-detail">Try UTF-8 first, fall back to latin-1 or cp1252. Use <code>chardet</code> library for auto-detection on unknown files.</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Column names</div><div class="step-detail">Strip whitespace, lowercase, replace special characters with underscores. Consistent naming makes downstream code easier.</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Inspect</div><div class="step-detail">Check shape, dtypes, null counts, and duplicates. This 30-second check saves hours of debugging later.</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Handle nulls</div><div class="step-detail">Drop all-NaN rows, fill numeric with median, fill categorical with a marker value. Strategy depends on your use case.</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Fix types + optimize</div><div class="step-detail">Parse currency strings, convert to category, downcast numerics. Reduces memory and catches data quality issues.</div></div></div><div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">6</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Export</div><div class="step-detail">Save as Parquet for your pipeline, CSV for sharing. Always verify the clean data before proceeding to analysis.</div></div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">COMPLETE PIPELINE FUNCTION</div><div class="example-body">Wrap the entire pipeline in a reusable function:<br><br><code>def load_and_clean(path, encoding="utf-8"):</code><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>df = pd.read_csv(path, encoding=encoding)</code><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>df.columns = df.columns.str.strip().str.lower().str.replace(r"[^a-z0-9]+", "_", regex=True).str.strip("_")</code><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>df = df.dropna(how="all").drop_duplicates()</code><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code># ... optimize dtypes ...</code><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>return df</code><br><br>This function becomes part of your personal toolkit. Every project starts with <code>df = load_and_clean("data.csv")</code>.</div></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Pipeline stages EN --- */
